@page "/components/calendar"

<PageTitle>Calendar — Unpoly.Blazor.Shadcn</PageTitle>

<h1 class="doc-h1">Calendar</h1>
<p class="doc-lede">
    A month, as a table — because a calendar is one, and every screen reader already knows how to
    read one. Each day is a real radio input, so choosing one posts and the browser supplies the
    arrow keys, Home and End within the group. No roving tabindex, no key handlers, no state.
</p>
<p class="doc-note">
    react-day-picker builds it from divs and then reimplements that, mostly correctly. What it
    also brings, and this does not, is the calendar systems — Hijri, Jalali — which are a data
    problem rather than a markup one.
</p>

<h2 class="doc-h2">Basic</h2>
<Example Source="CalendarBasic.razor"><CalendarBasic /></Example>

<h2 class="doc-h2">Date Picker</h2>
<p class="doc-note">
    You can use the <code>&lt;Calendar&gt;</code> component to build a date picker: put it in a
    <a href="/components/popover">Popover</a> and let the trigger show the chosen day. For an
    ordinary form field, <a href="/components/date-picker">DatePicker</a> around
    <code>&lt;input type="date"&gt;</code> is smaller and brings the OS picker on a phone; reach
    for the calendar when the shape of the month is part of the choice.
</p>
<Example Source="CalendarDatePickerExample.razor"><CalendarDatePickerExample /></Example>

<h2 class="doc-h2">Range Calendar</h2>
<p class="doc-note">
    One calendar, <code>Months="2"</code>, <code>Mode="range"</code>. With scripting off the days
    are checkboxes: tick the first and the last, and the server takes the min and the max — a
    working range picker before any JavaScript runs. The compiler only adds what a range picker
    should do beyond that: a third click starts again, and the band between the ends fills in.
</p>
<Example Source="CalendarRange.razor"><CalendarRange /></Example>

